<article class="env-lemma" id="0H7C">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0H7C">Lemma <span data-tag="0H7C">28.10.7</span>.</a> Let $x$ be a point of a locally Noetherian scheme $X$. Then $\dim _ x(X) = 0$ if and only if $x$ is an isolated point of $X$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      If $x$ is an isolated point, then $\{ x\} $ is an open subset of $X$ (Topology, Definition <a href="/tag/06RM" data-tag="06RM">5.27.2</a>) and hence $\dim _ x(X) = \dim (\{ x\} ) = 0$ by definition. Conversely, if $\dim _ x(X) = 0$, then there exists an open neighbourhood $U \subset X$ of $x$ such that $\dim (U) = 0$ (Topology, Definition <a href="/tag/0055" data-tag="0055">5.10.1</a>). By Lemma <a href="/tag/0AAX" data-tag="0AAX">28.10.5</a> we see that the topology on $U$ is discrete and hence $x$ is an isolated point. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>